  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The MALR pipeline is a logical extension of PathFinder-PRM's error-typed supervision (text-only) to the multimodal domain. Critically, the proposal misses Perceval (arXiv 2604.24583, April 2026), which already trains perception-centric PRMs with perception-intensive data and token-level perceptual error grounding. The 'Training VL-PRMs' paper (arXiv 2509.23250) already proposes 'perception-focused supervision' for multimodal PRM training. While MALR's specific multi-category error typing of training labels is technically novel, the conceptual contribution — decompose PRM supervision by modality — is the obvious next step in an extremely crowded field (5+ concurrent papers in May 2026 alone on perception-reasoning decomposition)."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "All required models (InternVL2.5-8B under MIT, Qwen2.5-VL-72B under research license) and datasets (VisualProcessBench, VisualPRM400K on HuggingFace) are available. GPU estimate (250-380 H100 hours, ~$625-$950) and annotation cost ($8K-$12K for 8K steps) are realistic. The >80% MALR-to-human agreement target on perception errors is ambitious given VLMs' own perception limitations, but achievable with careful engineering."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Exceptionally well-structured proposal with a precise 5-category error taxonomy, clear 3-stage MALR pipeline, and detailed evaluation plan spanning 5 benchmarks. The positioning against 20 references is thorough. However, the omission of Perceval and several other perception-credit-assignment papers (VICRA, AT-RL, PRCO) weakens the competitive landscape analysis."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "PathFinder-PRM — the closest text-only analogue — showed only modest improvements over binary PRMs (+2.2 PRMBench score, +1.5 prm@8). Unless multimodal error typing yields substantially larger gains (which is not guaranteed), the empirical impact may be insufficient for top venues. VisualProcessBench-Typed would be a useful community resource, but the core hypothesis (typed labels >> binary labels) carries significant empirical risk."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "Critical missing competitor: Perceval (arXiv 2604.24583, April 2026) trains perception-centric PRMs with perception-intensive data and performs token-level perceptual error grounding — it operates in the same 'perception-aware PRM training' space as MALR but is entirely uncited. The proposal must acknowledge and differentiate from Perceval explicitly.",
    "Narrower gap than claimed: 'Training VL-PRMs' (arXiv 2509.23250, Sep 2025) already proposes 'perception-focused supervision enabling PRMs to explicitly detect errors at the visual grounding stage' for multimodal PRM training. The claim that 'no work addresses perception errors in PRM training data' overstates the gap.",
    "Empirical risk from modest error-typing gains: PathFinder-PRM's text-only error typing yielded only +2.2 on PRMBench and +1.5 on prm@8 — modest margins. If multimodal error typing shows similarly marginal improvements over binary labels, the paper's central claim weakens substantially. The proposal needs a contingency argument for why multimodal gains should exceed text-only gains.",
    "MALR pipeline chicken-and-egg problem: Using Qwen2.5-VL-72B to verify visual premises in training labels while VLMs themselves have well-documented perception errors (shown by the very papers cited) creates a reliability ceiling. The pipeline's accuracy on perception error detection specifically — the hardest category — needs deeper analysis and fallback strategies.",
    "Overcrowded landscape risk: By ICLR 2027 submission (~Sep 2026), additional papers in this rapidly converging field (perception-aware PRM/reward training) will likely appear. The novelty window is closing; consider what differentiates MALR if 2-3 more perception-PRM papers appear by September 2026.",
    "Missing additional competitor papers: PRCO/'Seeing with You' (arXiv 2603.28618), VICRA/'Don't Lose Sight' (OpenReview), AT-RL/'Credit Where It's Due' (arXiv 2602.11455), and the 'Enhancing Multimodal LLMs via Perception Reward Modeling' (OpenReview W2pqpjROnf) all address modality-specific credit assignment and should be discussed in the landscape.",
    "AAAI 2027 stretch target is unrealistic: 9 weeks for annotation (3-4 weeks minimum), MALR pipeline engineering, PRM training experiments, evaluation across 5 benchmarks, and paper writing is not feasible even with compressed timelines."
  ],
  "feedback": "The idea is well-conceived and addresses a genuine gap, but Round 2 needs several targeted improvements: (1) **Cite and differentiate from Perceval** — this is the single most important revision. Perceval trains perception-centric PRMs but uses binary error detection focused on hallucinations, not multi-category error typing. Make this distinction explicit and argue why multi-category typing provides additional value over perception-only detection. (2) **Narrow and sharpen the gap claim** — acknowledge that perception-focused PRM supervision exists (Perceval, Training VL-PRMs) and reframe your contribution as 'multi-category modality-aware error typing' rather than 'first perception-aware PRM training.' (3) **Add an empirical motivation argument** for why multimodal error typing should yield larger gains than text-only (PathFinder-PRM's +1.5): e.g., multimodal reasoning has higher error diversity, perception errors are systematically different from reasoning errors in ways that flat binary labels conflate, the perception-reasoning correlation is lower in multimodal than text-only settings. (4) **Address the MALR reliability concern** — discuss what happens when Qwen2.5-VL-72B's own perception errors contaminate the MALR labels, and propose mitigation (e.g., ensemble of VLMs, confidence thresholds, human-in-the-loop for low-confidence perception verifications). (5) **Drop AAAI 2027** as a target — focus entirely on ICLR 2027 with the realistic 14-16 week timeline. (6) **Strengthen impact framing** — beyond VisualProcessBench-Typed, consider releasing the MALR pipeline as a tool and the typed VisualPRM400K subset, making the contribution more than just a paper.",
  "reason": "Solid idea with clear technical contribution, but missing a critical competitor (Perceval), overstating the gap, and facing significant empirical risk from an overcrowded landscape — average score 3.5/5 falls below the 4.0 threshold."
}
